Negative control for the cluster's rules (T2.1 · AC1.1 · D9).

Makes every rule unconditional. The bar still draws its three segment slots and
still uses the real `Separator.ce.vue` for the rules — the only thing removed is
the condition that a rule is only earned BETWEEN two segments that actually
rendered something. A segment renders nothing on its own account (`P13`, and any
page that registers no contexts), so under this mutant the bar trails a rule
against empty space, or doubles one around the segment that stayed empty.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: scope-bar.spec.ts — the case that a rule shows only between two rendered
segments (no rule at either end, exactly one between two neighbours whichever
segment between them drew nothing).

Mechanism note, not an assertion hint. The collapse is CSS, so it is observable
two ways and neither needs a browser: the rendered separator carries `hidden` as
its base with a single conditional variant, and that variant's selector is a
plain `querySelectorAll` away from being run against a hand-built bar — jsdom
matches `:empty` and `:has()` faithfully, and a Vue `v-if` comment placeholder
leaves its wrapper `:empty` exactly as an absent segment should. A gate that
asserts only the separator COUNT in the DOM cannot see this mutant at all: both
rules render either way, which is the whole point of collapsing them in CSS
rather than unmounting them.

